We will start with **direct substitution** (step A):

$\begin{align}
g(\blueD{0})&=\dfrac{\sin^2(2(\blueD{0}))}{1-\cos^2(\blueD{0})}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{0^2}{1-1^2}
\\\\
&=\dfrac00
\end{align}$

We got the **indeterminate form** $\dfrac00$ (step D).

Now we need to determine which analytical method is applicable.

Since we have a quotient with trigonometric expressions, we will use the method of substituting **trig identities** (step G):

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{\sin^2(2x)}{1-\cos^2(x)}&=\dfrac{\sin^2(2x)}{\sin^2(x)}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{4\cancel{\sin^2(x)}\cos^2(x)}{\cancel{\sin^2(x)}}
\\\\
&=4\cos^2(x)
\end{align}$

Now we can try **direct substitution** (step A) once again!
